  "opening": "In Chapter 5, we formulated quantum computation and traced circuits acting on a single qubit. Useful computations, however, require registers of many qubits. While Chapter 4 introduced the state space of a composite system, we now need to see how gates and measurements act on multi-qubit registers.",
  "opening_continuation": "We first describe multi-qubit states and gates using tensor products and distinguish independent qubits from entangled ones. We then explore the physical consequences of multi-qubit states for copying, communication, and evaluating functions.",
  "intro_continuation": "If two qubits are prepared independently in states \\(\\alpha\\ket{0}+\\beta\\ket{1}\\) and \\(\\gamma\\ket{0}+\\delta\\ket{1}\\), how are the four amplitudes of the combined register related to the amplitudes of the individual qubits—and can every two-qubit state be formed this way?"
}
